Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:48:31 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 45/71] Price reasoning retention against the prompt that was
 actually sent

The pass took the first item history drops as the cache break and treated
every reasoning item after it as free to drop. But an item the previous
prompt already left out is not a break. After compaction the memory turn's
request survives as a user message that every prompt drops, so from then on
every reasoning item was "free", was dropped, and caused the very break it
was supposed to ride on: one session paid a 57,000-token re-prefill at 0%
cached to drop 16,013 tokens of reasoning at a horizon of one request.

The context manager now records which items each prompt carried, and the
pass prices against that. Only a carried item this prompt leaves out is a
break, only carried items count towards a re-prefill, and reasoning no
prompt has carried yet (the turn's last request wrote it) is dropped for
free, which at a horizon of zero is what stops the memory turn from paying
to carry it through every request it makes.

Also corrects the cached input price: Z.ai bills a cached token at a fifth
of a fresh one, not a tenth, so every verdict was twice as willing to keep.

